        "\n",
        "# Logic\n",
        "\n",
        "## Truth\n",
        "\n",
        "Make at least 3 observations about the following table:\n",
        "\n",
        "::::: columns\n",
        "::: {.column width=\"30%\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "| $p$ | $q$ | $p \\lor q$ |\n",
        "|---|---|:--------:|\n",
        "| F | F |   F    |\n",
        "| F | T |   T    |\n",
        "| T | F |   T    |\n",
        "| T | T |   T    |\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.column width=\"70%\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "*  \n",
        "\n",
        "\\\n",
        "\n",
        "*   \n",
        "\n",
        "\\\n",
        "\n",
        "*      \n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        ":::::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Summary\n",
        "\n",
        "Key observations about the table:\n",
        "\n",
        "::::: columns\n",
        "::: {.column width=\"30%\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "| $p$ | $q$ | $p \\lor q$ |\n",
        "|---|---|:--------:|\n",
        "| F | F |   F    |\n",
        "| F | T |   T    |\n",
        "| T | F |   T    |\n",
        "| T | T |   T    |\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.column style=\"width: 70%; font-size: 80%;\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "*   The diagram is called a _Truth Table_\n",
        "*   p and q are _variables_, each of which take on one of two _Boolean_ values, True or False (T/F, 1/0) \n",
        "*   ∨ is a _binary operator_ that implements a function (creates output) from the 2 input variables to another Boolean value. It is characterized by its output values.\n",
        "*   The operator ∨ corresponds to the English word _or_. \n",
        "*   The expression p ∨ q is called a _proposition_.\n",
        "    \n",
        ":::\n",
        ":::::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Foreshadowing {.activity}\n",
        "\n",
        "::::: {.columns}\n",
        "::: {.column width=\"30%\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "| $p$ | $q$ | $p \\lor q$ |\n",
        "|---|---|:--------:|\n",
        "| F | F |   F    |\n",
        "| F | T |   T    |\n",
        "| T | F |   T    |\n",
        "| T | T |   T    |\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.column style=\"width: 70%;\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "*   How many rows for a proposition on 3 variables? $k$ variables?\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "*   Give a good name for an operator that takes 1 variable: __________\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "*   Give a good name for an operator that takes 3 variables: __________\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "*   How many different binary operators could there be? \n",
        " \n",
        ":::\n",
        ":::::\n",
        "\n",
        "{{< countdown minutes=10 >}}\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Truth Tables {.activity}\n",
        "\n",
        "All graded learning activities in the course will use a tool called _PrairieLearn_. \n",
        "\n",
        "Navigate to today's activity and complete question 1\n",
        "\n",
        "{{< qrcode https://us.prairielearn.com/pl/course_instance/186238/assessment/2562335 >}}\n",
        "\n",
        "<https://us.prairielearn.com/pl/course_instance/186238/assessment/2562335>\n",
        "\n",
        "{{< countdown minutes=10 >}}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Beyond Operators\n",
        "\n",
        "_Propositions_ can be more complex than just a single operator! \n",
        "\n",
        "Example:\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "| $p$ | $q$ | $\\neg(p \\lor q)$ |\n",
        "|---|---|:--------:|\n",
        "| F | F |   T    |\n",
        "| F | T |   F    |\n",
        "| T | F |   F    |\n",
        "| T | T |   F    |\n",
        "\n",
        "::: notes\n",
        "<!-- so what kinds of expressions CAN we make? -->\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## WFFs\n",
        "\n",
        "A _well-formed formula_ is a Boolean statement generated by the following rules:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{bnf}\n",
        "<wff> ::= <atom>\n",
        "        | ~ <wff>\n",
        "        | ( <wff> ∧ <wff> )\n",
        "        | ( <wff> ∨ <wff> )\n",
        "\n",
        "<atom> ::= p | q | r | s | ...\n",
        "\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "Speculate:\n",
        "\n",
        "*\n",
        "*\n",
        "*\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## WFFs\n",
        "\n",
        "A _well-formed formula_ is a Boolean statement generated by the following rules:\n",
        "\n",
        "```{bnf}\n",
        "<wff> ::= <atom>\n",
        "        | ~ <wff>\n",
        "        | ( <wff> ∧ <wff> )\n",
        "        | ( <wff> ∨ <wff> )\n",
        "\n",
        "<atom> ::= p | q | r | s | ...\n",
        "\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "This definition is in the form of a _Grammar_. \n",
        "\n",
        "_____________: `<wff>` and `<atom>`\n",
        "\n",
        "_____________: `p`, `q`, `r`, `s` ...\n",
        "\n",
        "It is our first self-referential or _recursive_ definition. \n",
        "\n",
        "## WFFs\n",
        "\n",
        "```{bnf}\n",
        "<wff> ::= <atom>\n",
        "        | ~ <wff>\n",
        "        | ( <wff> ∧ <wff> )\n",
        "        | ( <wff> ∨ <wff> )\n",
        "\n",
        "<atom> ::= p | q \n",
        "\n",
        "```\n",
        "Example derivation:\n",
        "\n",
        "`<wff>`\n",
        "\n",
        "## WFFs (Notes)\n",
        "\n",
        "```{bnf}\n",
        "<wff> ::= <atom>\n",
        "        | ~ <wff>\n",
        "        | ( <wff> ∧ <wff> )\n",
        "        | ( <wff> ∨ <wff> )\n",
        "\n",
        "<atom> ::= p | q \n",
        "\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "* Grammars can be used to construct _many_ different kinds of sequences. \n",
        "\n",
        "* We could have included additional operators $\\rightarrow$, $\\leftrightarrow$, $\\oplus$, $\\uparrow$\n",
        "\n",
        "* Computational evaluation of `<wff>`is covered in DSCI221. For now, we trust Python and focus on logic.\n",
        "\n",
        "* The `<wff>` are _propositions_. \n",
        "\n",
        "## WFF Puzzle {.activity}\n",
        "\n",
        "Which of these are WFFs?\n",
        "\n",
        "::::: {.columns}\n",
        "::: {.column width=\"50%\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "1. $(\\neg p ∧ (q ∨ r))$  \n",
        "2. $((p ∧ q) ∨ (r ∧ \\neg s))$ \n",
        "3. $((\\neg p) ∨ (q ∨ r))$ \n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "::: {.column width=\"50%\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "4. $(p ∨ (qr))$\n",
        "5. $\\neg (p ∨ (q ∧ \\neg r))$\n",
        "6. $\\neg (p ∨ q))$\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        ":::::\n",
        "\n",
        "{{< countdown minutes=5 >}}\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "::: notes\n",
        "<!-- notes: sometimes pq is the same as p^q, p & q, p && q, but our grammar only \n",
        "uses ^. In this course we will vary symbols fluidly, partly because we're \n",
        "assembling info from many different sources, but also partly because you'll\n",
        "need to be agile in the future. Note that the same issues exist for other operators. -->\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## WFFs\n",
        "\n",
        "```{bnf}\n",
        "<wff> ::= <atom>\n",
        "        | ~ <wff>\n",
        "        | ( <wff> ∧ <wff> )\n",
        "        | ( <wff> ∨ <wff> )\n",
        "\n",
        "<atom> ::= p | q \n",
        "\n",
        "```\n",
        "Theorem: $\\neg (p ∨ q))$ is not a WFF.\n",
        "Proof:\n",
        "\n",
        "::: notes\n",
        "<!-- assume that it is! then ~<wff> must have been the last rule applied, \n",
        "and $( p v q))$ must be a <wff>. then ( <wff> v <wff> ) must have been the \n",
        "last rule applied, and $p$ and $q)$ must be <wff>. take each case separately:\n",
        "$q)$ is not a <wff> because none of the rules apply, so we have both that it \n",
        "must be a wff and it is not a wff -- a contradiction, so our assumption \n",
        "is false!  \n",
        "\n",
        "We include this as foreshadowing for a proof strategy called \"proof by \n",
        "contradition.\"\" Making arguments like this (proofs) is the substance of this course, \n",
        "but don't worry, we'll be teaching you how to do it. -->\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Propositions\n",
        "\n",
        "A _proposition_ is a statement that can be either **True** or **False**. \n",
        "\n",
        "Examples:\n",
        "\n",
        "* $37 > 12$\n",
        "\n",
        "* Fewer than 5 people in this room feel sleepy.\n",
        "\n",
        "* There are extra-terrestrial life forms. \n",
        "\n",
        "* This statement is False. \n",
        "\n",
        "* $\\underline{\\hspace{10em}}$\n",
        "\n",
        "## Translation\n",
        "\n",
        "\\\n",
        "\n",
        "$p$: ___________ ate cereal for breakfast.\n",
        "\n",
        "$q$: ___________ brought a backpack to class.\n",
        "\n",
        "\\ \n",
        "\n",
        "* $(p\\lor q)$\n",
        "\n",
        "* $(p\\wedge q)$\n",
        "\n",
        "* $\\neg q$\n",
        "\n",
        "## Predicates\n",
        "\n",
        "\\\n",
        "\n",
        "$p$: ___________ ate cereal for breakfast.\n",
        "\n",
        "\\\n",
        "\n",
        "We may want to apply the statement to many students, in which case we define a \n",
        "_Predicate_.\n",
        "\n",
        "\\\n",
        "\n",
        "* $P(x)$: $x$ ate cereal for breakfast.\n",
        "\n",
        "* $x$ can be _instantiated_ to be a particular student, or an arbitrary student.\n",
        "\n",
        "* $P(\\underline{\\hspace{2em}} )$ is a proposition. \n",
        "\n",
        "## Logical Equivalence\n",
        "\n",
        "\\\n",
        "\n",
        "Ex: Is it true that $(p \\lor q) \\equiv (q\\lor p)$ ?\n",
        "\n",
        "\\\n",
        "\n",
        "Discussion points:\n",
        "\n",
        "* $\\equiv$ means _logically equivalent_\n",
        "\n",
        "* The answer had better be _________!!!\n",
        "\n",
        "* How can we justify our instinct?\n",
        "\n",
        "::: notes\n",
        "<!-- 1) show same truth table 2) chains of equivalence -->\n",
        "<!-- same as saying \"write a proof\" -->\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Logical Equivalence example\n",
        "\n",
        "{{< video https://youtu.be/CLQ0LZSnJFE?si=jHDBkjZ2OnkfQqwW width=\"850\" height=\"475\">}}\n",
        "\n",
        "## Boolean Masks\n",
        "\n",
        "Select all the cereals with at least 4 units of protein and no more than 6 units of sugars.\n",
        "\n",
        "```{pyodide}\n",
        "#| setup: true\n",
        "#| exercise: ex_cereal\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "# import numpy as np\n",
        "\n",
        "# Load and prepare data\n",
        "cereals = pd.read_csv('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/UBC-CS/dsci220/refs/heads/main/data/cereal.csv')\n",
        "# cereals = cereals.dropna()\n",
        "\n",
        "```\n",
        "\n",
        "```{pyodide}\n",
        "#| exercise: ex_cereal\n",
        "p = cereals['protein'] < 4 \n",
        "q = cereals['sugars'] > 6\n",
        "filtered_df = cereals[______]\n",
        "filtered_df\n",
        "```\n",
        "```{pyodide}\n",
        "#| exercise: ex_cereal\n",
        "#| check: true\n",
        "correct_answer = cereals[(cereals['protein'] >= 4) & (cereals['sugars'] <= 6)]\n",
        "if len(filtered_df) == len(correct_answer) and all(filtered_df.index == correct_answer.index):\n",
        "        feedback = {\n",
        "        \"correct\": True,\n",
        "        \"message\": \"Perfect! You correctly filtered for healthy cereal.\"\n",
        "        }\n",
        "else:\n",
        "        feedback = {\n",
        "        \"correct\": False,\n",
        "        \"message\": \"Not quite. Make sure you're using both conditions: protein of at least 4 and sugars of at most 6.\"\n",
        "        }\n",
        " feedback\n",
        " ```\n",
        "\n",
        "::: notes\n",
        "<!-- note that by doing this filtering, we are defining a predicate instantiated by\n",
        "each row whose truth value is evaluated. -->\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Logical Equivalence\n",
        "\n",
        ":::: smaller\n",
        "\n",
        "\"Select all the cereals with at least 4 units of protein and no more than 6 units of sugars.\"\n",
        "\n",
        "|        $p$: a cereal has less than 4 units of protein\n",
        "|        $q$: a cereal has more than 6 units of sugars\n",
        "\n",
        "We suspect that $\\neg p\\wedge \\neg q \\equiv \\neg( p\\lor q)$:\n",
        "\n",
        "\\\n",
        "\n",
        "::::\n",
        "\n",
        "::::: {.columns}\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.column style=\"width: 50%; font-size: 80%;\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "| $p$ | $q$ | $\\hspace{4em}\\neg p\\wedge \\neg q$ |\n",
        "|---|---|--------|\n",
        "| F | F |       |\n",
        "| F | T |       |\n",
        "| T | F |       |\n",
        "| T | T |       |\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.column style=\"width: 50%; font-size: 80%;\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "| $p$ | $q$ | $\\hspace{4em}\\neg( p\\lor q)$ |\n",
        "|---|---|:--------:|\n",
        "| F | F |       |\n",
        "| F | T |       |\n",
        "| T | F |       |\n",
        "| T | T |       |\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        ":::::\n",
        "\n",
        "::: notes\n",
        "<!-- half the class complete the TT on the left, the other half on \n",
        "the right, then we all read our responses aloud! -->\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Words and Ideas \n",
        "\n",
        "Truth Table\n",
        "\n",
        "Proposition\n",
        "\n",
        "Grammar\n",
        "\n",
        "Predicate\n",
        "\n",
        "Boolean Mask\n",
        "\n",
        "Logical Equivalence\n"
      ],
